# =============================================================================
# Technical Knowledge Assistant — configuration reference
# =============================================================================
#
# READ THIS FIRST — three things that are easy to get wrong:
#
#   1. A LOCAL RUN NEEDS NONE OF THIS. Every variable below has a working
#      default in code. With nothing set, the application selects SQLite,
#      local vector similarity, local Ollama, in-memory conversation state,
#      image reading off, and no hosted tracing.
#
#          pip install -r requirements.txt
#          python -m assistant.indexing.index
#          python -m assistant.interfaces.ui
#
#   2. THERE IS NO DOTENV LOADER IN THIS REPOSITORY. Copying this file to
#      `.env` does NOT configure a local `python -m ...` run. Nothing reads
#      `.env` except Docker Compose. To change a setting locally, export it
#      in your shell first:
#
#          PowerShell:  $env:ASSISTANT_VISION_DEMO = "1"
#          bash:        export ASSISTANT_VISION_DEMO=1
#
#   3. THIS FILE IS A REFERENCE, NOT A TEMPLATE TO COPY WHOLESALE. Every
#      variable in the local sections below is commented out on purpose:
#      uncommenting one without changing its value changes nothing, because
#      it already restates the code's own default. Uncomment only to move
#      AWAY from the default.
#
# Never commit `.env`. Only `.env.example` is tracked.
#
# Deploying with containers? Use `deploy/compose.yaml` and
# `deploy/.env.example` instead — see the PRODUCTION section at the bottom,
# which explains why the root `docker-compose.yml` is not the one to use.
#
# Every default below was read out of the code, and the file that owns it is
# named so you can check rather than trust.


# =============================================================================
# 1. STORAGE  — the one switch that changes the shape of the system
# =============================================================================

# Knowledge store selection. This is the ONLY storage switch, and it is read at
# every entry point: indexer, CLI, web UI, session store and readiness check.
#   owner: assistant/knowledge/store/factory.py
#
#   unset or empty  ->  SQLite at data/index/knowledge.db
#                       No server to start, no pgvector, runs offline. This is
#                       the assessment/demonstration path.
#   a DSN           ->  PostgreSQL + pgvector
#                       See the PRODUCTION section.
#
# ASSISTANT_POSTGRES_DSN=

# Conversation checkpointing. NOT the same variable as the one above, and the
# distinction is deliberate: knowledge and conversation state are different
# stores with different lifetimes.
#   owner: assistant/answering/engine.py -> assistant/turn/graph.py
#
#   unset or empty  ->  LangGraph InMemorySaver. State lives in the process,
#                       does not survive a restart, is not shared between
#                       workers. This is the intended local behaviour.
#   a DSN           ->  RAISES PostgresCheckpointerUnavailable. It does not
#                       silently fall back, and that is the point: a deployment
#                       that believed its conversations were durable and quietly
#                       lost them would be worse than one that refuses to start.
#                       The blocker is a version conflict, not a decision —
#                       langgraph-checkpoint-postgres 3.0.1 requires
#                       langgraph-checkpoint<4, while langgraph 1.2.11 requires
#                       >=4.1.0. See DECISIONS.md entry 20.
#
# ASSISTANT_CHECKPOINT_DSN=

# Content-addressed embedding cache. Ships with the repository (it is committed
# deliberately — DECISIONS.md entry 17), so a clean clone indexes in seconds
# instead of the ~15 minutes the embeddings would otherwise cost on CPU.
# Keyed by SHA-256 of the passage text plus the model tag and dimension count,
# so it either hits correctly or misses and recomputes — a cached vector from a
# different model can never be returned.
#   owner: assistant/indexing/embedcache.py   (default: data/embeddings.db)
#
# ASSISTANT_EMBEDDING_CACHE=data/embeddings.db


# =============================================================================
# 2. MODELS  — all served by local Ollama; no API key, no hosted service
# =============================================================================
#   owner: assistant/infrastructure/ollama.py

# Where Ollama is listening. Note 127.0.0.1, not a container hostname: inside
# Docker Compose this must be the service DNS name `ollama:11434`, because
# localhost inside a container is the container.
# OLLAMA_HOST=http://127.0.0.1:11434

# The generation model. Used for composition and for structured model tasks
# (query understanding, vision perception). It runs on ONE answer path; extract,
# route, refusal and hand-off use no model at all.
# GENERATION_MODEL=qwen3.5:4b

# The embedding model and its vector width. Both are recorded in the index
# header, and the engine REFUSES to query an index built with a different model
# rather than returning confident nonsense — so changing either of these means
# rebuilding the index, and forgetting to is not silently possible.
#
# Note this is a development default, not a final selection: DECISIONS.md entry
# 6 says the embedding model is chosen by measurement, and
# `python -m eval.embedding_choice` is that measurement. It has not been run.
# EMBED_MODEL=qwen3-embedding:0.6b
# EMBED_DIMENSIONS=1024

# How long Ollama keeps the model resident after a call. Measured on the build
# machine: 8.11 s cold against 0.72 s warm, so a reload costs ~7.4 s and lands
# on whoever asks the first question after a quiet spell. The trade is roughly
# 3 GB of resident memory for a predictable first answer.
#   5m  restores Ollama's own default
#   0   unloads as soon as the call returns
# OLLAMA_KEEP_ALIVE=30m

# Context window, for generation AND for vision. One variable drives both, and
# that is deliberate rather than lazy: Ollama keys a resident model instance on
# the context size, so two different sizes for one model are two instances, and
# a request for the second against the first blocks indefinitely instead of
# erring or resizing.
# OLLAMA_NUM_CTX=8192


# =============================================================================
# 3. IMAGE READING  — off by default, and that is a supported state
# =============================================================================
#   owner: assistant/answering/vision.py

# Turns image reading on. Accepts 1/true/yes/on; anything else is off.
#
# OFF IS NOT BROKEN. With it off, an uploaded photograph is acknowledged and
# handed to a person — which is the published behaviour DECISIONS.md entry 16
# describes. Only coverage changes, never safety.
#
# Off is the default for three reasons. Perception costs one to three minutes
# per image on a processor with no graphics card (measured: 191.9 s for one
# 512x512 image), so an accidental upload on a shared deployment is a
# denial-of-service with good intentions. The vision model is a separate pull
# an operator may not have. And a confident wrong visual reading remains the
# worst failure this system can produce — which is why, even with this on,
# inside-or-outside still cannot be decided from a photograph.
# ASSISTANT_VISION_DEMO=1

# Vision model tag. Defaults to the generation model, which must then be
# multimodal. Only read when ASSISTANT_VISION_DEMO is on.
# VISION_MODEL=qwen3.5:4b

# --- Vision tuning. Defaults are measured, not guessed; change with care. ---

# Upload ceiling in bytes (default 8 MiB). A phone photograph is a few
# megabytes; anything much larger is a mistake or an attempt to make the machine
# do arithmetic on a gigabyte, and both are refused at the boundary.
# VISION_MAX_IMAGE_BYTES=8388608

# Seconds allowed for one perception call. Long, because a vision encoder on a
# CPU is slow — see the measurement above.
# VISION_TIMEOUT=600

# Token ceiling, and the number of observations kept. Both exist because of what
# the first real call did: asked to describe one image, the model fell into a
# repetition loop and emitted 37 near-identical observations. There are fourteen
# attributes and no photograph honestly settles fourteen things.
# VISION_MAX_TOKENS=900
# VISION_MAX_OBSERVATIONS=6

# Sampler repeat penalty, against that same loop. Temperature zero makes a model
# deterministic, not sensible: with no penalty the likeliest continuation after
# an observation is often the same observation again. Kept mild — a large
# penalty would discourage the legitimately repeated structure of a JSON array.
# VISION_REPEAT_PENALTY=1.15


# =============================================================================
# 4. OPTIONAL BEHAVIOUR
# =============================================================================

# Wording polish. Takes an answer that has ALREADY passed every check and asks
# the model to say the same thing in a more direct order, keeping the rewrite
# only if it can prove nothing material moved — same citation markers, same
# figures, no new claims. The model sees no evidence, only the question and the
# verified answer, so there is nothing in front of it to launder into a new fact.
#
# Off by default because it is a second model call, and on CPU a second call
# costs what the first one costs.
#   owner: assistant/answering/phrasing.py
# ASSISTANT_PHRASING=1


# =============================================================================
# 5. TELEMETRY
# =============================================================================

# --- Hosted LangSmith/LangChain tracing: NOT configurable here, on purpose ---
#
# Do not add LANGCHAIN_TRACING_V2 or LANGSMITH_* to this file — they will not
# work. `langgraph` depends on `langchain-core`, which depends on `langsmith`,
# a client for a hosted tracing service that exports whole runs — and a run here
# contains the customer's question, the retrieved passages and the conversation
# state.
#
# It is closed in code, by ASSIGNMENT rather than setdefault, in
# `assistant/__init__.py` before anything imports it, and again at the
# process-global level in `assistant/turn/graph.py`. The earlier version used
# `setdefault`, which by definition does not override, and a parent environment
# carrying LANGCHAIN_TRACING_V2=true re-enabled export. For this variable, in
# this application, there is no operator choice to respect.
#
# `tests/test_privacy_tracing.py` launches the application under hostile
# environment values and proves tracing stays off, with a control test proving
# those same values WOULD switch it on without this application — so the suite
# cannot pass by guarding nothing.

# --- OpenTelemetry export: the only telemetry this project sanctions ---
#   owner: assistant/infrastructure/otel_export.py
#
# Opt-in. An empty endpoint disables export entirely, which is the default.
# Examples:
#   http://localhost:4317                                  local collector
#   https://otlp.datadoghq.com                             Datadog
#   https://otlp-gateway-prod-us-west-0.grafana.net/otlp    Grafana Cloud
#
# Note: `deploy/compose.yaml` does NOT currently pass these into the app
# container, so setting them in `deploy/.env` has no effect there. They work for
# a local run when exported in your shell.
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=
# OTEL_SERVICE_NAME=technical-knowledge-assistant
# OTEL_SERVICE_VERSION=1.0.0
# OTEL_DEPLOYMENT_ENVIRONMENT=development


# =============================================================================
# 6. PRODUCTION / CONTAINERS
# =============================================================================
#
# WHICH STACK TO USE: `deploy/compose.yaml` with `deploy/.env`.
#
#     cp deploy/.env.example deploy/.env      # then set POSTGRES_PASSWORD
#     docker compose -f deploy/compose.yaml up -d
#
# The root `docker-compose.yml` and root `Dockerfile` are an earlier stack and
# are superseded. Nothing builds them except this comment's warning: CI builds
# `deploy/Dockerfile`, and both verification scripts target `deploy/`. Prefer
# `deploy/` for anything real.
#
# Neither compose file declares `env_file`, so each picks up the `.env` sitting
# NEXT TO IT: `docker-compose.yml` reads `./.env`, and `deploy/compose.yaml`
# reads `deploy/.env`. That is why there are two example files, and why the
# values below differ from `deploy/.env.example` — they configure different
# stacks. Do not merge them.

# --- PostgreSQL + pgvector ---------------------------------------------------
#
# The adapter is implemented and passes the same behavioural repository contract
# as SQLite, against a real pgvector instance in CI. It is contract-verified
# rather than operated: the demonstration runs on SQLite, and no PostgreSQL
# instance has answered a question outside a test. Do not read a variable's
# presence here as evidence that the capability is in production use.
#
# Inside Compose use the service DNS name `db:5432`, never localhost.
# ASSISTANT_POSTGRES_DSN=postgresql://postgres:postgres@db:5432/knowledge_assistant

# Consumed by the ROOT docker-compose.yml to build the database service and the
# DSN above. Read by the application only through that DSN — no Python code
# reads these four directly.
POSTGRES_DB=knowledge_assistant
POSTGRES_USER=postgres
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=postgres
POSTGRES_PORT=5432

# --- Model server inside Compose (service DNS, not localhost) ---
OLLAMA_HOST=http://ollama:11434
OLLAMA_PORT=11434

# --- Application container ---
#
# NOTE: APP_PORT is a Compose host-port mapping ONLY. No application code reads
# it. The web server's port is a command-line option (`--port`, default 8765),
# not an environment variable. LOG_LEVEL is likewise read by nothing in this
# codebase — both are kept here because the root compose file interpolates them,
# and are flagged so nobody sets one expecting it to take effect.
APP_PORT=8000
LOG_LEVEL=INFO
PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1
